<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#patroon">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Patroon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1629">1629</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Patroonship</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A patroon was a landholder in New Netherland granted a large estate, a patroonship, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> under its Charter of Freedoms and Exemptions of 1629, in return for settling fifty adults there within four years. Patroons held manorial rights over their tenants, including courts. Most patroonships failed, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rensselaerswyck">Rensselaerswyck</a>, founded by Kiliaen van Rensselaer, prospered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/patroon.jpg" alt="Patroon" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chartered West India Company (West-Indische Compagnie) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charter_of_Freedoms_and_Exemptions_(Dutch_West_India_Company)_1630.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A patroonship could stretch 16 miles along one bank of a major river, or 8 miles if it spanned both banks. The title carried wide powers: a patroon could set up civil and criminal courts, appoint local officials and hold his land in perpetuity. His first settlers were tenants who paid him rent but were freed from public taxes for ten years, and a patroonship sometimes had its own village and churches.</p><p>The charter also entitled patroons to acquire enslaved labour through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>. Patroons, often the richest and most influential people of New Netherland, held enslaved people in almost every part of the colony and were essentially its only slaveholders, the largest numbers living near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a> and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-orange">Fort Orange</a> and Rensselaerswijck.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In 1640 the charter was revised to halve the size of new grants and to let any New Netherlander in good standing buy an estate. The early patents fared badly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwaanendael">Zwaanendael</a> on Delaware Bay, held by Samuel Blommaert and Samuel Godyn, was abandoned after an attack by its Indigenous neighbours; Pavonia, in today's Hudson County, was sold back to the company by Michael Reyniersz Pauw; Cornelis Melyn's Staten Island grant was mired in conflict with the Raritan; and Achter Col on the Hackensack River was given up at the outbreak of Kieft's War. Rensselaerswijck, founded by Kiliaen van Rensselaer, covered almost all of present-day Albany and Rensselaer counties and parts of Columbia and Greene counties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the English took New Netherland in 1664, and again after American independence, large tracts were granted as manors, among them Fordham Manor in the Bronx in 1671 and Robert Livingston the Elder's Livingston Manor of 160,000 acres. The word patroonship was used until 1775, when the British parliament redefined the lands as estates and took away their courts; many Dutch Americans resented this, and the new state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> did not reverse it.</p><p>By the end of the 18th century nearly every American state had abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a> and entail, so the old manors became large estates open to division. Rensselaerswijck was broken up in the early 19th century after the death of its last sole proprietor, Stephen Van Rensselaer III, when tenant farmers won the right to refuse rent and his sons sold much of the land. Resistance to the system is remembered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leislers-rebellion">Leisler's Rebellion</a> and the Anti-Rent War.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Patroon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
